Kane-Mele on the honeycomb at delta = 0 and rashba = 0, PRL 95, 226801 (2005):
one monolayer, whose horizontal mirror is unbroken, asked three ways.

A. the layer in the plane, declared free-standing
   realized group   : #191.234 P6/mmm1', 4 bands, orbitals at Wyckoff 2c
                      carrying the site irrep -E1
                      (free-standing is a declaration: a flat model has no third
                      coordinate, and a layer on a substrate has no mirror in z)
   valence content  : GM: -GM9   K: -K8   M: -M6
   the EBR rows     : the class of a layer needs the 11 k_z = 1/2 rows the plane
                      has no coordinate for; they are measured on the layer's own stack
   class            : (1, 0), order 6 in X_BS(#191.234) = Z6 x Z12

B. the same layer, with the physical p_z declared
   realized group   : #191.234 P6/mmm1', orbitals at Wyckoff 2c
                      carrying the site irrep -E2
                      (the declared p_z is odd under the mirror, m(z) = -1, and
                      an even s orbital lifts to -E1 instead)
   valence content  : GM: -GM12   K: -K9   M: -M5
   class            : (5, 0), order 6 in X_BS(#191.234) = Z6 x Z12
   inversion        : #191.234 has r -> -r, the product of the mirror m(z) and the
                      twofold rotation C2z; its spinor lift squares to +E, so its
                      eigenvalues are +-1 and a parity count exists, while the lifts of
                      m(z) and C2z square to Ebar with eigenvalues +-i.
                      C2z acts on in-plane k as inversion does but is a rotation: this
                      inversion is the layer group's and needs the third direction.
                      It exchanges the two honeycomb sites: site 0 -> 1, site 1 -> 0.
   rashba = 0.25    : the same call measures #183.186 P6mm1', a subgroup of index 2.
                      #191.234 has operations with R[2,2] = -1 and +1; what the Rashba
                      term leaves has only R[2,2] = +1 -- the mirror and everything
                      composed with it is gone, and X_BS(#183.186) = trivial

C. the layer as a periodic decoupled stack
   the stack        : 3 periodic directions and no vacuum, period c = 8
   no k_z           : the stack's H(k, k_z) equals the layer's H(k) at k_z = 0, 0.25, 0.5,
                      max |H(k, k_z) - H(k)| = 0.000000 over 6 random in-plane k
   realized group   : #191.234 P6/mmm1', orbitals at Wyckoff 2c, k-points A GM H K L M
   six-point content: GM: -GM12   A: -A12   K: -K9   H: -H9   M: -M5   L: -L5
   class            : (5, 0), order 6 in X_BS(#191.234) = Z6 x Z12

D. the three classes side by side
   A. the plane, s          : #191.234, Z6 x Z12, class (1, 0), order 6
   B. the plane, p_z        : #191.234, Z6 x Z12, class (5, 0), order 6
   C. the stack, p_z        : #191.234, Z6 x Z12, class (5, 0), order 6
   the invariant part: order 6/6/6, z2w,3 = 1/1/1, z4 = 2/2/2
   B and C are one crystal presented two ways and their classes agree entry for
   entry; A and B differ in the declared site character alone, and that moves
   the component values and not the invariant part
   irreptables forms : z2w,3 = 1, z4 = 2, z3m,π = 2, z3m,0 = 2,
                       z6m,0 = 1, z6m,π = 1, z12 = 2
   out of the plane  : z2w,3, z3m,π, z6m,π read only the k_z = 1/2 rows, and
                       z4, z12 both planes.  Those rows repeat the in-plane ones,
                       so the class is the class of the layer's decoupled stack,
                       and every form is an invariant of that stack, fixed by
                       the layer's own content
   the control       : the four bands together are the band representation of the
                       orbitals at 2c, an atomic limit by construction, and their
                       class is (0, 0), every irreptables form 0

E. the layer's bands, and the stack's at k_z = 0 and k_z = 1/2

Done — figure written to <FIGDIR>
